WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.420
Turn a newt's eye upside down and let its nerve regrow.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.760
Why does it never learn to aim again?

3
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:14.053
In 1943, Roger Sperry rotated the eyes of adult newts.

4
00:00:14.793 --> 00:00:16.933
He cut the optic nerve and let it regrow.

5
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:22.213
When sight returned, a lure held above its head made it turn its head downward.

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:27.603
If the fibres regrew at random and use then matched them, what should weeks of

7
00:00:27.603 --> 00:00:28.603
missed prey teach?

8
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:37.293
To aim correctly. It never learned.

9
00:00:38.093 --> 00:00:40.653
It kept making the mistake for as long as anyone watched.

10
00:00:43.683 --> 00:00:48.683
Sperry favoured the simplest reading: each regrown fibre went back to its old place

11
00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:49.683
in the brain.

12
00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:52.043
That place is part of a map.

13
00:00:53.063 --> 00:00:56.563
Neighbouring cells in the retina connect to neighbouring places in the target.

14
00:00:57.583 --> 00:01:01.063
Temporal retina, the side nearer the temple, maps to the front.

15
00:01:02.043 --> 00:01:03.523
Nasal retina maps to the back.

16
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:08.717
A growth cone, the moving tip of an axon, senses only what is close.

17
00:01:09.677 --> 00:01:10.937
Yet the map spans the target.

18
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:14.087
So how does an axon know where to stop?

19
00:01:15.127 --> 00:01:16.127
Three ways are possible.

20
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.660
Labels: one matching tag for each cell and each target site.

21
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.560
Gradients: a smooth slope in the amount of a molecule gives each position a value.

22
00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:32.133
Neighbours: each axon places itself relative to the axons around it.

23
00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:36.907
In chick and mouse, the retina uses gradients and neighbours together.

24
00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:41.067
Each axon carries an amount of a receptor called EphA.

25
00:01:41.907 --> 00:01:45.067
The target carries a slope of a repellent, ephrin-A.

26
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.060
The repellent is low at the front and high at the back.

27
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.740
The axon with the most receptor ends where it is lowest.

28
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.630
More receptor, less ligand.

29
00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:59.770
But where each axon lands also depends on the other axons.

30
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:08.310
The takeaway: an axon's place is not an address in its own receptor level.

31
00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.870
It is a rank, set against a slope.
